{
  "id": "COMPOUND:43386 - PROTEIN:6138",
  "verbalization": "Nardostachin appears to have multiple targets that ultimately lead to the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. The first pathway links Nardostachin to Lysine-specific demethylase 4D-like, which is targeted by a compound called 5alpha,6alpha-Epoxy(22e,24r)-ergosta-8(14),22-dien-3beta,7alpha-diol. This compound, in turn, targets the 26S proteasome subunit, suggesting a chain of interactions involving enzymatic activity. The second and third pathways connect Nardostachin to Microtubule-associated protein 1B, which is targeted by Cassialactone and CID 11603911, both of which also target the 26S proteasome subunit. These findings suggest that Nardostachin may act through different molecular mechanisms, but all ultimately converge on the 26S proteasome regulatory subunit. The evidence supports a potential role for Nardostachin in modulating proteasome function, possibly through the activation or inhibition of its non-ATPase regulatory subunit. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, with the proteasome subunit serving as a common endpoint across multiple targets.",
